<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yinxu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1300–1046 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century-bce">14th century BCE</a> · Also known as Ruins of Yin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yinxu, the 'Ruins of Yin', is the site of the last capital of the Shang dynasty, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anyang">Anyang</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, occupied from about 1300 BCE until the Zhou conquest. Excavations since 1928 have revealed palace and temple foundations, royal tombs, bronze workshops, the tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fu-hao">Fu Hao</a> and tens of thousands of inscribed oracle bones. It became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2006.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yinxu.jpg" alt="Yinxu" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tim Wu · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Exterior,_Yinxu_Museum_20241002-B.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
